<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-randomness" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Probability and Hypothesis Testing</title>
  <p>
    Now I have defined a null hypothesis and an alternative hypothesis
    are. A research study wants to determine if the alternative
    hypothesis holds—if the alternative hypothesis can be rejected.
    The way to do this is using probability. This section explains
    that setup.
  </p>
  <p>
    I’ll use the same example as in <xref ref="section-hypotheses"/>
    about promotion decisions and gender. This was the research
    question: under a male manager, is there a prejudice against women
    in promotion decisions? From the data, there was a a sample
    statistic: men were promoted at at rate of <m>87.5%</m> and women
    at a rate of <m>58.3%</m>. The difference was <m>29.2</m>, or
    <m>0.292</m> as a proportion.
  </p>
  <p>
    So here is the main question: what is the probability that the
    sample statistics was just due to randomness? The sample
    statistics shows a difference, but maybe this difference is just
    random variance. If I flip a fair coin ten times, I might very
    well get <m>7</m> heads and <m>3</m> tails. That would be a
    difference in the sample, but it would just be the product of
    random variation. The coin is still fair, and flipping the coin
    more times would eventually demonstrate that (due to the Law of
    Large Numbers).  Likewise, maybe these particular promotion
    decisions, which do show a difference, are just some random
    variation since the sample is not the whole population.
  </p>
  <p>
    This gets back to the hypotheses. The null hypothesis says there
    is no relationship in the parameter, in the whole population. A
    sample can still be different, since a sample is a small piece of
    the population. If the null hypothesis is true, then what is the
    probability of producing a sample statistics like the one that we
    have?
  </p>
  <p>
    This is what we do. We assume the null hypothesis is true. Then,
    under that assumption, we determine the probability of producing
    the sample statistics. Then we evaluate this probability: if this
    probability is very small, then it is very unlikely that the
    sample statistics was randomly produces, which means that it is
    very unlikely that the null hypothesis is true. Let me give you
    some definitions for this setup.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A research study has to set a threshold of significance.
        This is a probability which is small enough to consider it
        unlikely to have been produced randomly. The conventional
        value here is <m>5\%</m>, but that is only a convention —
        the threshold of singnificant can be set as the researches
        wish). Then, a sample statistics is called significant is
        the probability of producing the sample statistic, under the
        assumption of the null hypothesis, is less than the
        threshold of significant. This probability, that the sample
        statistics can be produced under the null hypothesis, is
        called the <m>p</m>-value of the sample statistic.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    If a sample statistics is significant, then we are justified in
    rejecting the null hypothesis and, therefore, supporting the
    alternative hypothesis. This is how we test a hypotheiss: we set a
    threshold of significant, use mathematics to determine the
    probability of producing the sample statistics under the null
    hypothesis, and compare to the threshold to determine significant.
    If significant, we reject the null hypothesis and conclude that
    the sample supports the alternative hypothesis.
  </p>
  <p>
    In <xref ref="section-probability-theory"/> I said that there were
    two ways to establish a probability: theory or simulation.
    Eventually, in this course, we will rely heavily on the
    mathematical theory of probability. But I do also want to do some
    simulations, to show you how that works.
  </p>
  <p>
    So let me return to the example. Temporarily ignoring the gender,
    of the <m>48</m> cases in the study, <m>35</m> were promoted and
    <m>13</m> not promoted. Of the promotion applicants, <m>24</m>
    were men and <m>24</m> were women. In the sample statistic,
    <m>21</m> men were promoted and 14 women (adding together to make
    the total <m>35</m>).
  </p>
  <p>
    I can make a simulation by randomly assigning these <m>35</m>
    promotions to the applicants. I asked a random number generate to
    flip a coin <m>35</m> times. For every <m>1</m>, I recorded a
    promotion for one of the women. For every <m>0</m>, I recoreded a
    promotion for one of the men.  (If I happend to get to <m>24</m>
    promotions for either of the categories, then I assigned the
    remainder to the other category).  That gives me a random
    assignment of the promotions. I did this ten times. The follwing
    table shows the results, including the calculation of the
    difference: proportion of men promotion minus proportion of women
    promoted.
  </p>
  <table xml:id="table-promotion-random">
    <title>Ten Random Promotion Simulations</title>
    <tabular>
      <row bottom="medium">>
        <cell> Male </cell>
        <cell> Female </cell>
        <cell> Promotion </cell>
        <cell> Difference </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> Randomization 1</cell>
        <cell> 20 </cell>
        <cell> 15 </cell>
        <cell> 0.208 </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> Randomization 2</cell>
        <cell> 22 </cell>
        <cell> 13 </cell>
        <cell> 0.357 </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> Randomization 3</cell>
        <cell> 15 </cell>
        <cell> 20 </cell>
        <cell> -0.208 </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> Randomization 4</cell>
        <cell> 20 </cell>
        <cell> 15 </cell>
        <cell> 0.208 </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> Randomization 5</cell>
        <cell> 11 </cell>
        <cell> 24 </cell>
        <cell> -0.542 </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> Randomization 6</cell>
        <cell> 19 </cell>
        <cell> 16 </cell>
        <cell> 0.125 </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> Randomization 7</cell>
        <cell> 20 </cell>
        <cell> 15 </cell>
        <cell> 0.208 </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> Randomization 8</cell>
        <cell> 14 </cell>
        <cell> 21 </cell>
        <cell> -0.292 </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> Randomization 9</cell>
        <cell> 18 </cell>
        <cell> 17 </cell>
        <cell> 0.042 </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> Randomization 10</cell>
        <cell> 12 </cell>
        <cell> 23 </cell>
        <cell> -0.458 </cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>
  <p>
    Then I compare this to the sample statistic: <m>0.292.</m> In nine
    of these ten randomizations, the difference in proportions was
    less that <m>0.292</m>.  In one randomization, the second one, the
    difference in proportions was larger. Therefore, from this
    particular set of randomizations, I would conclude that there is a
    <m>10\%</m> chance of producing the sample statistic. If the null
    hypothesis is true, then my sample statistic has probability
    <m>10\%</m>. That is the <m>p</m>-value. <m>p = 0.10</m>.  (Like
    most percentages,<m>p</m> -values are almost always expressed as
    proportions of one. Then I would consider the threshold of
    signification and decide if this <m>10\%</m> counted as
    significant.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now, doing ten randomization is not very many. This is probably
    not a great simulation and this probability is probably incorrect.
    However, if I did this randomanization thousands and thousands of
    times, then the Law of Large Numbers tells me that I should
    actually get a very accurate idea of the probability.
  </p>
  <p>
    So, I run many, many random trials. Then I look at my sample
    statistic: a <m>0.292</m> different in the proportion of men and
    women promoted. Then I ask, how often does this happen? How often
    do at least <m>29.2%</m> more men get promoted than women if the
    promotions were split up randomly? I haven’t shown the work of
    this many randomzation, but the answer, after all the random
    trials, is about <m>2\%</m>.  The <m>p</m>-value is <m>p =
    0.02</m>.  If I use the conventional threshold of <m>5\%</m> then
    <m>p \lt 0.05</m> so this is a significant result. I conclude that
    I can reject the null hypothesis and support the alternative
    hypothesis. This sample does support the idea that there is, in
    fact, a gender bias in bank promotions under male managers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Evaluation of statistics is all about what is likely. We approach
    truth by probability and approximation. Statistics doesn’t
    conclude that a claim is true or false; it concludes that a claim
    is likely or unlike. As such, it always involves a judgement call,
    yet again.  
  </p>
  <p>
    But this is how hypothesis testing works. A sample statistic is
    generated from a sample. Some math or random trials are done to
    figure out if the probability of the sample statistic being just
    random, just noise. If that probability is low enough, if it is
    very unlikely that the sample statistic is just random change,
    then it is called statistcally significant, the null hypothesis is
    rejected, and the study gives real evidence for the alternative
    hypothesis.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, this whole setup of hypothesis testing only works if the
    data is good data. All of the constructions here, about
    probability and randomness and significant, assume that the data
    is realiable. If the data is compromised, due to bad data
    collection, human error, bias, or any other cause, then no amount
    of good mathematics and probability theory will solve that
    problem. We can only do mathematics to produce significant on good
    data; bad data remains unrealiable regardless of how excellent our
    understanding of probability theory is.
  </p>
</section>

